 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "vn-earlylondon-close-the-centre-before-it-is-challenged",
   "line": "d4 Nf6 Bf4 c5 d5",
   "teaches": "When the bishop comes out before the pawns are settled, a pawn push in the centre answers the challenge by closing it — judge the push by the two squares it covers rather than by the space it takes, because with both entry squares held the pieces opposite have nowhere to develop toward.",
   "explains": "The pawn has been offered on c5 and the centre pawn advances past it to d5, covering c6 and e6. Those are the squares the knight and the light-squared bishop want. The position locks, which suits the side whose bishop is already outside the pawn chain — the piece that could have been trapped is the one with the open diagonal.",
   "reasons": [
